# cp, as ipnxbuild's build steps see it: /bin/cp, except that a failed
# link's output is never copied.  ipnxbuild puts this first on PATH, named cp,
# around its steps and nowhere else; every rule gets it without being edited.
#
# WHY.  ld(1): `This file is made executable only if no errors occurred during
# the load.'  A program whose link failed is therefore still there, mode 664,
# and an install rule's cp put it in place.  Over a working binary it arrived
# executable, because cp keeps an existing target's mode (cp/cp.c:88 is
# creat(to, mode), and only a NEW file takes that mode), and a rule that
# chmods afterwards makes even a new one runnable.  Measured: the trek a
# missing gtty left answered `Illegal instruction - core dumped' before
# printing anything (docs/v10-gaps.md).
#
# WHAT A FAILED LINK LOOKS LIKE, measured rather than assumed -- the first
# version of this guard looked for ld's default 0413 and passed every failed
# link it was shown.  An undefined symbol makes ld keep the relocation bits
# and write OMAGIC (ld.c:847-858: `rflag++' then `sflag = zflag = 0'), so a
# failed link is a 0407 image, the format of an object file: `cc -o bad' over
# one undefined name left 637 bytes of 0407 with 72 bytes of text relocation,
# where the same program linked is 3,408 bytes of 0413.  So what is refused is
# a source with NO execute bit, a VAX a.out magic -- 0407, 0410 or 0413 in the
# low half of the first long and 0 in the high half; a PDP-11 image's second
# word is its text size -- and a name that ends in neither .o nor .a.  Every
# object this build copies is a .o but two, which are bare objects named as
# libraries: libdbm.a (`mv dbm.o libdbm.a', libdbm/mkfile:4) and libsdb.a.
# The first version of this guard exempted .o alone and refused those two, the
# only things it matched in a full build -- every refusal is in build.log, so
# that is a complete list.  The tree holds no other VAX image (checked from the
# host, 8 Oct 2026), so nothing else can match.  The rule's
# own `|| echo' then says FAILED, ipnxbuild's count has it, and the old target
# is left whole.  The mode comes from ls, not `test -x': v10 answers yes for
# root whatever the bits say -- measured, a 664 copy of /bin/ls.
#
# The last argument is the destination and is never inspected; an option
# (cp(1) has only -z) is not a source.
p=
for a
do
	if test -n "$p" -a -f "$p"
	then
		case $p in
		*.o|*.a)	;;
		*)	case `ls -l "$p"` in
			???[xs]*|??????[xs]*|?????????[xt]*)	;;
			*)	case `od -o "$p" | sed 1q` in
				'0000000 000407 000000 '*|'0000000 000410 000000 '*|'0000000 000413 000000 '*)
					echo "cp: $p: a failed link's output -- ld(1) left it unexecutable -- not copied" 1>&2
					exit 1 ;;
				esac ;;
			esac ;;
		esac
	fi
	case $a in
	-*)	p= ;;
	*)	p=$a ;;
	esac
done
exec /bin/cp "$@"
